/*!
    \page ai-assistance.html
    \title Asking an AI for Help

    The \l{Utils::AiAssistance} API connects two kinds of plugins:

    \list
        \li Plugins that want an AI to do something, such as to explain an
            issue or to write a commit message. They describe the task in an
            \l{Utils::AiRequest}.
        \li Plugins that integrate an AI, such as the ACP Client. They derive
            from \l{Utils::AiProvider} and offer targets, such as the agents
            that the user configured.
    \endlist

    Neither side depends on the other, as both use the API in the Utils
    library. A plugin that wants help works the same whether no AI plugin is
    loaded or several are. A built-in provider copies a request to the
    clipboard, so the user can always paste it into an AI that \QC does not
    know.

    \section1 Requesting Help

    An \l{Utils::AiRequest} holds the prompt and the context that goes along
    with it, such as files, lines in a file, text, and images.

    \code
    using namespace Utils;

    AiRequest request;
    request.id = "MyPlugin.ExplainSelection";
    request.title = Tr::tr("Explain Selection");
    request.prompt = "Explain what the attached code does.";
    request.context.append(AiContextItem::fromFile(filePath, range));
    request.context.append(AiContextItem::fromText("Build output", output));

    AiAssistance::submit(request);
    \endcode

    \l{Utils::AiAssistance::submit()} lets the user pick a target in a dialog,
    and preselects the target that the user picked for a request with the same
    \c id the last time. To pass a target without asking, use the overload
    that takes an \l{Utils::AiTargetSelection}.

    To offer the request in a menu, \l{Utils::AiAssistance::createAction()}
    creates an action that builds the request when the user triggers it, and
    that is disabled while no target is available.

    \section2 Interactive and Headless Requests

    By default, a request is \l{Utils::AiRequest::Interactive}{interactive}:
    the target takes it over, and the user continues there, for example in a
    chat with an agent.

    A \l{Utils::AiRequest::Headless}{headless} request reports the answer back.
    Only targets that \l{Utils::AiTarget::supportsHeadless}{support} it are
    offered for such a request, and \l{Utils::AiAssistance::hasTargets()} tells
    whether there are any.

    \code
    request.mode = AiRequest::Headless;
    AiResponse *response = AiAssistance::submit(request);
    connect(response, &AiResponse::finished, this, [this](const Result<QString> &answer) {
        if (answer)
            useAnswer(*answer);
    });
    \endcode

    \l{Utils::AiResponse} follows the request. It reports the answer in
    pieces with \c textReceived() as the target produces it, and ends with
    \c finished() exactly once. \c finished() is never emitted before the
    event loop runs, so connecting to it right after submitting is in time.
    The response deletes itself afterwards. To stop a request, call
    \l{Utils::AiResponse::cancel()}.

    \section2 Requests in Task Trees

    \l{Utils::AiRequestTask} runs a request as a task of a task tree, for
    example after the task that collects its context. Stopping or canceling
    the tree cancels the request.

    \code
    const Storage<QString> diff;
    const auto onAiSetup = [diff](AiRequestRunner &runner) {
        AiRequest request;
        request.prompt = "Write a commit message for the attached diff.";
        request.mode = AiRequest::Headless;
        request.context.append(AiContextItem::fromText("Diff", *diff));
        runner.setRequest(request);
    };
    const auto onAiDone = [this](const AiRequestRunner &runner) {
        useAnswer(runner.result().value_or(QString()));
    };
    const Group recipe {
        diff,
        ProcessTask(onDiffSetup, onDiffDone),
        AiRequestTask(onAiSetup, onAiDone)
    };
    \endcode

    \section2 Offering a Button

    \l{Core::AiTaskButton} is a button with the icon of the AI chat that runs
    such a recipe. While it runs, the button shows a spinner and the progress
    shows in the status bar. Clicking the button again or canceling the progress
    cancels the task tree. The button is hidden while no target can run its kind
    of request.

    \code
    auto button = new Core::AiTaskButton;
    button->setRequestMode(AiRequest::Headless);
    button->setIdleToolTip(Tr::tr("Let an AI write the commit message."));
    button->setProgress(Tr::tr("Generating Commit Message"), "MyPlugin.GenerateMessage");
    button->setRecipe([this] { return commitMessageRecipe(); });
    \endcode

    To put the button next to the title of a group box, use
    \l{Utils::attachToGroupBoxTitle()}.

    \section1 Providing an AI

    A provider registers itself when it is created and unregisters when it is
    deleted. Create it when the plugin initializes, and delete it with the
    plugin.

    \code
    class MyAiProvider final : public Utils::AiProvider
    {
    public:
        MyAiProvider()
            : AiProvider("MyPlugin.MyAi", Tr::tr("My AI"))
        {
            AiTarget target;
            target.id = "default";
            target.displayName = Tr::tr("My AI");
            target.supportsHeadless = true;

            AiTargetParameter model;
            model.id = "model";
            model.displayName = Tr::tr("Model");
            model.category = AiTargetParameter::Model;
            model.choices = {{"small", Tr::tr("Small"), {}}, {"large", Tr::tr("Large"), {}}};
            model.defaultValue = "small";
            target.parameters = {model};

            setTargets({target});
        }

        void execute(const AiRequest &request, const AiTargetSelection &selection,
                     AiResponse *response) final
        {
            // Run the request with the model in selection.parameters.value("model"),
            // stream the answer with response->reportText(), and end with
            // response->reportFinished().
        }
    };
    \endcode

    A provider can change its targets at any time with the protected function
    \c setTargets(), for example when it learns which
    models an agent offers only after talking to it. Open dialogs follow the
    change.

    In \l{Utils::AiProvider::execute()}, a provider should stop working on the
    request when \l{Utils::AiResponse} emits \c cancelRequested(). If the AI
    only takes plain text, \l{Utils::AiAssistance::toPlainText()} turns the
    request with its context into a text that quotes the lines of files.
*/
